WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
Good morning John. I've been spending a

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.669
lot of time going through the archives

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:03.959
of the Vlogbrothers channels, as we're

00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:05.759
doing our This Day in Vlogbrothers thing.

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:07.259
There's a link in the description to that if you're

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:08.460
curious what I'm talking about. But I've noticed

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.690
the vast majority of Question Tuesday

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.490
videos, they go to you John, and while

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:12.389
this makes a certain amount of sense

00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:14.309
because you make videos on Tuesdays, I

00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:15.630
think it's a little bit unfair. So today

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.070
is Question Tuesday on a Friday. I

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.539
asked people on YouTube and Facebook and

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:21.029
Twitter to ask me hard questions and

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:22.199
here's the ones I got. Are you going to

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.600
answer this question first? Joe, that was

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.760
pretty easy actually. What is the new

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.650
Republican healthcare plan all about?

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.720
This—this is a hard one because there

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.520
isn't a new Republican healthcare plan.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.870
They just want to keep all the good

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.090
parts of Obamacare but make it cost

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:37.649
less by waving their hands around. But

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:38.790
if you want to get a little bit less of

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.920
a partisan hot take on this one,

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.020
Healthcare Triage has an amazing and

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.210
in-depth video on this. Is flying to space

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.070
the fastest way for Superman to fly? You

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.480
know, I don't know too much about the

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.250
physics of how Superman's supposed to work.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.199
So if, like, if air resistance is a

00:00:52.199 --> 00:00:54.329
really big deal for Superman, then maybe?

00:00:54.329 --> 00:00:55.890
But my feeling is that air resistance probably

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.390
shouldn't matter to Superman too much, it's

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.739
not like he's gonna get too hot,

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:00.329
he seems pretty impervious. So probably no,

00:01:00.329 --> 00:01:01.800
it's gonna take longer, but the further

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.329
your radius is, the longer your

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.129
circumference is, that's—that's how

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:07.110
circles work. But I am not Superman's

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.450
keeper. He can do it however he wants to

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.369
do it. How do you properly pronounce "thistle"?

00:01:11.369 --> 00:01:13.020
You know, I'm not gonna lie to you, I don't know how to

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.670
pronounce anything. Every time I get a

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.020
little bit confused I just open up

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.330
this app, "How J Say." He's just a man who

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.369
happens to have said, like, every word, so...

00:01:20.369 --> 00:01:28.490
J: Thistle. 
What, I mean, what do you want J to say?
J: Butt. Butt. Butt. Butt. Butt. Anglerfishes. Pooping.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:30.380
Sorry, J. You're my whole world. Thank

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.840
you for doing you that great work.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.280
When people come to VidCon, do they typically rent

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:35.810
a car? I don't know if they do, but they

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:37.460
shouldn't, because parking in Los

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.530
Angeles is terrible and so is driving.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:40.790
Get a cab to your hotel. You can probably

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.320
walk to the convention center. If you need

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:43.939
to, you can get an Uber anytime. How do

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:45.560
you eat a pear without getting pear juice all

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.149
up on your nose? Annelise, I'm a huge

00:01:47.149 --> 00:01:49.130
connoisseur of pears. I love them all.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:50.360
Sometimes I want to get it all over.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.009
Sometimes I want that. But if I don't, I get

00:01:52.009 --> 00:01:53.869
out a nice sharp knife and I just, zoop,

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:55.850
right down and I make a little square of

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.649
the core and then I'm like, nibble on the

00:01:57.649 --> 00:01:59.509
core, then just into strips and eat

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:02.509
them, and oh, it's so good and mess free.

00:02:02.509 --> 00:02:04.190
You have to clean the knife, and also maybe your

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.600
plate that you put them on. But it's a

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.729
wonderful ritual way to spend

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:10.069
time, the feeling of the knife going just

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:12.860
like through that soft, soft Bartlett,

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:16.069
yeah! I may get too excited about pears. I

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:17.480
just realized that for the first time.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.090
Would Bernie have won? For this question,

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:21.680
let's have a guitar.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.840
♪ Well I don't know and nobody else does ♪

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.000
♪ and if you think that you're sure then you're wrong ♪

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.300
♪ It's too easy to be certain of things that can't be proven ♪

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:32.030
♪ so when you feel that way, sing this song ♪

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:33.860
How's Dave? Well, if you're talking about the

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.740
fish, Dave is less good... He can

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:39.470
barely hold up his own string, but he's

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:41.810
still floatin'. How many cats is too many cats?

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.480
Well, according to my cat, two cats would

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.280
be too many cats, which is terrible for

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:47.870
me because my actual answer is like

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:50.959
maybe eight? I like cats. And pears. ♪ Cat

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:53.930
pineapple apple pear. ♪ Is the S or the C

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:56.510
silent in "scent"? Oh, that's weird, because

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.870
"cent" and "sent" and "scent" are all words.

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:01.310
All right, I looked it up on the Online

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:02.870
Etymology Dictionary, says that the C

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:04.850
in "scent" was added in the 17th

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.370
century, because people FELT LIKE IT! So

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:08.300
it's the C,

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.100
I think, that's silent. Can this be the last

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.980
question? Yes. I have here with me,

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:15.980
additionally, a bunch of the prints that

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.230
Claire Emery made for us to celebrate our

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:21.110
10th year anniversary on YouTube. Hello

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:25.310
llama! Yeah! I got Claire to loan me this so

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:26.600
that I could show it off. This is the

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:29.120
actual wood block that is put into the

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.910
press, and these actually degenerate as

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:33.200
they are used, and so there's only a

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:34.610
certain amount before they lose their

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.079
crispness and you just can't use them

00:03:36.079 --> 00:03:37.850
anymore. So when you do woodblocks,

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.489
it's definitionally a limited run. I love

00:03:40.489 --> 00:03:41.989
them. They smell good.

00:03:41.989 --> 00:03:43.549
I didn't know that they would smell good.

00:03:43.549 --> 00:03:45.290
But that's the thing. I'm—I just wanted

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.660
to show you me holding them, in real life,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.180
so that you can see the paper, and what

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:51.769
it looks like, and how big they are, and

00:03:51.769 --> 00:03:53.180
stuff. There's a link in the description

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:54.470
if you're interested in these. Thanks for

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:56.720
all the questions, and John, I'll see you

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:02.530
on Tuesday.

